WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.540
Viewing The Flintstones through adult eyes
reveals a complex world with a lot of dark

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.860
or problematic moments.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.880
You wouldn't notice them if you're a kid,
but if you're a grown up...you yabba-dabba-do.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.240
While The Flintstones is clearly a riff on
the groundbreaking sitcom The Honeymooners,

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.180
it does make a few notable changes.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.300
The Flintstones is a bit more of an equal
opportunity offender when it comes to domestic

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:22.820
abuse.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.619
Ralph Kramden threatened to beat his wife
so often that his verbal promise of violence

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:28.880
became a TV catchphrase…

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:29.860
"Bang.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.360
Zoom."

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.540
Audiences never actually saw Ralph get physical
with his wife but viewers of The Flintstones

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.960
saw it work the other way around.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.160
Fred often threatened Wilma, but that's where
it stopped.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:46.120
However, when Fred did something Wilma didn't
like, which was often, she'd clock him on

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.380
the head, drop heavy objects on his feet,
fling him across the room, and who knows what

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:50.880
else.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.720
"Wilma, no, not in the head!"

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.300
Of course, Fred wasn't the easiest caveman
to live with.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.460
He had serious anger management issues, he
constantly twisted the truth and his views

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.560
on the role of women in society really did
belong in the Stone Age.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.780
"How many times have I told you, a woman's
place is in the home!"

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.899
For a show that took place millions of years
ago, The Flintstones is surprisingly progressive

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.229
about a number of issues.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.500
In the 1960s, it was revolutionary that the
series was open about how Barney and Betty

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.810
Rubble couldn't naturally conceive a child
like the Flintstones, and how upset they were

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:32.300
about it.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:38.920
It was a major TV event in February 1963 when
Wilma gave birth to Pebbles, and afterwards,

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:42.749
the Rubbles spend an inordinate amount of
time hanging out with the new arrival, they

00:01:42.749 --> 00:01:44.540
just want a baby so bad.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.670
As a last resort, they make a wish on a falling
star.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.560
The next morning, their wish has apparently
been granted and they find a baby boy at their

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.909
doorstep…a supernaturally strong orphan
named Bamm Bamm.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:56.909
"Wheeee!

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:59.860
Oh, Barney, we've got our very own baby."

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.660
However, the couple still had a long way to
go before they could call Bamm Bamm their

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.160
own.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.820
Adult viewers will certainly feel some empathy,
as the Rubbles jump through legal hoops to

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.820
adopt the little powerhouse, and are only
granted custody at the last minute.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.500
The whole ordeal mentally drains Barney to
the point where he wants to take his own life.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.720
"Goodbye cruel world."

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.400
Luckily, Fred shows up just in time to save
the day and Bamm Bamm's new daddy.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.000
The Flintstones wouldn't be The Flintstones
without animals, birds, and other creatures

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.980
performing all kinds of household and mechanical
tasks.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.200
The modern Stone-Age family seems to have
an animal for every job, a mastodon instead

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:42.640
of a shower, a monkey/bird tag team inside
a record player, a swordfish for a kitchen

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.160
knife, and an artistic bird that functions
as a prehistoric Polaroid camera, just to

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:47.660
name a few.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.060
You have to wonder what these animals actually
think of Fred.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.600
"The guy's a pig, a neanderthal!

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.720
"You're dead to me, can opener!"

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.090
It doesn't seem like the animals on The Flintstones
are getting paid, or have any freedom whatsoever.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.820
In other words, the humans of The Flintstones
have enslaved animals, forcing all creatures

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.460
great and small into a being beasts of burden.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.620
While this is cruel, some animals have it
worse than others.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.840
Birds' tails are constantly being yanked.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.920
A pelican serves as a trash can, meaning people
force-feed it garbage.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.980
And, possibly worst of all, a variety of poor
creatures are forced to sacrifice their entire

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.020
lives for construction and mining.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.660
"I've had just about enough of these lazy
f---s using us as slave labor!"

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:31.900
"The uprising is upon us!"

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.120
Like most sitcoms of the 1960s, The Flintstones
was not serialized.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.860
Each episode completely stood on its own,
with Fred, Wilma, Barney, and Betty getting

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.800
into some kind of hairy situation and resolving
it by the end of the episode.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.180
Kids can accept this.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.300
They can enjoy shows on a surface level.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.400
Adults may want to know more about developments
in The Flintstones universe, such as what

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.000
happens after the events in the episode "Dino
and Juliet."

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.240
In this comic version of Romeo and Juliet,
Fred Flintstone and a new neighbor get into

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.480
a heated dispute.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.400
Instead of the warring factions' teenage offspring
falling in love, their pet dinosaurs do.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:13.080
Dino falls instantly head over heels for Juliet,
the cute new sauropod in town.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.460
"Well if that don't beat the bush!

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.020
Dino's in love!"

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.139
As lovers are wont to do, Dino and Juliet
steal away for some privacy...which results

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.080
in the birth of more than a dozen babies.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.600
Never again on The Flintstones are Juliet
or her children with Dino seen.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.340
Apparently, he's an absentee father and the
love-'em-and-leave-'em type.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.419
After a few years on the air, sitcoms tend
to bring in a new character to breathe fresh

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.560
life into the series.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.860
Homeless teen Luke showed up on Growing Pains,
Cousin Oliver appeared on The Brady Bunch,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.740
and The Simpsons mocked the entire concept
with a random character named Roy.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.540
"Adding a new character is often a desperate
attempt to boost low ratings."

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:54.720
"Yo, yo!

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.300
How's it hanging, everybody?"

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.560
"Morning, Roy."

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:03.999
The Flintstones pioneered this move back in
1965 with the arrival of a tiny, smug alien

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.560
named the Great Gazoo.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.710
The little green E.T. had been sent through
space and time to prehistoric Earth as punishment

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.139
for making a doomsday device on his home planet.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.800
Gazoo clearly has seen his share of dark places.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.680
"Get off me, you big lummox!"

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.680
"Gazoo!

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:20.520
Where have you been?"

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.960
Fred and Barney discover him, and rather than
report him to the government, they befriend

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.090
him, although Gazoo mainly seems to call them
"dumb-dumbs" and magically disappear whenever

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:33.400
Fred is talking to him, which gives the impression
that Fred has mental issues.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:39.020
Or maybe the Great Gazoo isn't real at all...and
Fred and Barney have been ingesting some unknown

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.020
substances with mysterious side-effects.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.279
Yes...The Flintstones is a cartoon, and it
passed strict network standards in the conservative

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.400
early 1960s.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.200
But, somehow, it managed to sneak in the occasional
dirty joke for the adults in the room.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.200
In one episode, Fred and Barney are watching
TV when they lose reception.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.420
Fred asks if everything is in working order
and Barney immediately hits Fred with a comeback

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.000
that would make most parents blush in front
of their kids.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.260
"How's your antennae?"

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.260
"Fine, Fred.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:06.820
How's yours?"

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.900
It would also seem that they had "adult films"
way back in the Stone Age.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.280
While getting a check-up, a doctor holds up
Fred's x-ray, prompting Flintstone to joke,

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.140
"How about it, Doc?

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.260
Flintstone in an x-ray-ted picture!"

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.999
While these man-cave jokes were right up Fred
and Barney's alley, we can't help but wonder

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.720
how Wilma and Betty would react.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.160
"Bang."

